<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee Stack</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Lee Stack</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Lee Stack was a British army officer who served as Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and Sirdar of the Egyptian Army from 1917. He was shot by Egyptian nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in November 1924 and died the next day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> responded with an ultimatum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the expulsion of Egyptian troops and officials from the Sudan, strengthening British control of the condominium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-stack.jpg" alt="Lee Stack" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wide World Photos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Major_General_Sir_Lee_Stack_1924.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
